    },
    {
      "filename": "Quest Field (2890744955).jpg",
      "title": "File:Quest Field (2890744955).jpg",
      "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/06/Quest_Field_%282890744955%29.jpg",
      "descriptionUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Quest_Field_(2890744955).jpg",
      "width": 960,
      "height": 1280,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "CC BY 2.0",
      "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/2.0",
      "artist": "Cliff from Arlington, Virginia, USA",
      "description": "Within Qwest Field, there is a Seahawks tribute to high school football of the State of Washington. A section of the Stadium entitled \"The State of Football\", features a large depiction of the State of Washington and holds replica football helmets from every high school football team in the State. Qwest Field has earned a reputation as arguably the loudest stadium in the NFL. Allen had the architects design the structure of the stadium, especially the roof, to direct as much crowd noise as possible on the field. In addition, the north end zone seating, called the \"Hawks Nest\", was specifically designed for rowdy fans; the seating consists of metal bleachers which reflect sound, and fans often stomp to create even more. Seahawks fans already had a reputation for being among the most vociferous in the NFL; while they played in the Kingdome, opposing teams often practiced with rock music blaring at full blast to prepare for the often painfully high decibel levels typical of Seahawk games.",
      "dateCreated": "2008-09-26 23:26",
      "categories": [
        "2008 in Seattle",
